/** @page dataframe_typed_examplesguide Typed DataFrame Examples

    @tableofcontents

    DataFrame methods that produce another frame now declare
    @ref Qore::DataFrame::DataFrame "DataFrame" directly, and grouped operations
    declare @ref Qore::DataFrame::GroupedDataFrame "GroupedDataFrame" or
    @ref Qore::DataFrame::DataFrame "DataFrame" according to the value returned.
    Methods that return fixed record shapes use typed hashdecls:

    @code{.py}
%requires dataframe

DataFrame::DataFrame employees(
    ({"dept": "sales", "name": "Alice", "salary": 65000.0},
     {"dept": "sales", "name": "Bob", "salary": 70000.0},
     {"dept": "eng", "name": "Carol", "salary": 95000.0}));

hash<DataFrame::DataFrameShape> shape = employees.shape();
printf("rows=%d cols=%d\n", shape.rows, shape.cols);

DataFrame::DataFrame sales = employees.filter("dept", "==", "sales");
DataFrame::GroupedDataFrame grouped = employees.groupBy(("dept",));
DataFrame::DataFrame means = grouped.mean();

list<hash<DataFrame::ColumnStats>> stats = employees.describe();
foreach hash<DataFrame::ColumnStats> stat in (stats) {
    printf("%s: %s count=%d\n", stat.name, stat.dtype, stat.count);
}
    @endcode

    DataFrame also registers native plugin-operator operations for common
    access patterns. Row slices use normal inclusive Qore range semantics,
    including open-ended forms:

    @code{.py}
list<auto> names = employees["name"];
hash<auto> second_row = employees[1];
DataFrame::DataFrame first_two = employees[0..1];
DataFrame::DataFrame reversed = employees[2..0];
DataFrame::DataFrame from_second = employees[1..];
    @endcode

    Predicate masks use explicit column references so direct column access stays
    simple and predictable. Compare a @ref Qore::DataFrame::ColumnRef "ColumnRef" with
    a scalar to get a @ref Qore::DataFrame::RowMask "RowMask"; then pass the mask to
    \c frame[mask] or @ref Qore::DataFrame::DataFrame::filter(Qore::DataFrame::RowMask) "filter(mask)".
    The comparison operators are registered plugin operations and work in AST,
    IR, and JIT execution. In JIT/AOT-generated code, the hot subscript,
    comparison, and mask-composition operators use direct module-helper calls
    while preserving the same runtime-helper fallback behavior:

    @code{.py}
DataFrame::ColumnRef salary = employees.column("salary");
DataFrame::RowMask high_salary = salary >= 90000.0;
DataFrame::DataFrame senior = employees[high_salary];

DataFrame::DataFrame sales = employees.filter(employees.column("dept") == "sales");
DataFrame::DataFrame known_salary = employees[employees.column("salary") != NOTHING];

printf("selected %d of %d rows\n", high_salary.count(), high_salary.size());
    @endcode

    Supported predicate operators are \c ==, \c !=, \c <, \c <=, \c >, and
    \c >= against scalar \c int, \c float, \c number, \c string, \c bool,
    \c date, \c null, and \c NOTHING values. \c NOTHING equality selects null
    rows; \c NOTHING inequality selects non-null rows. Ordered comparisons with
    \c NOTHING do not select rows.
*/
